 "char": {
  "id": "dot2",
  "handle": "dot_on_the_dial",
  "name": "Dot",
  "bio": "Forty years of listening to the sky.",
  "voice": "af_sarah",
  "case": "starfish",
  "look": {
   "skin": "#e8c4a8",
   "hair": "#d9d6cf",
   "style": "bob",
   "shirt": "#3b5b7a",
   "glasses": true,
   "extra": "headphones"
  },
  "script": "My dad was in Honolulu on July ninth, 1962. Around eleven at night, the sky turned green, then blood red. America had set off a hydrogen bomb four hundred kilometres up, in space. They called it Starfish Prime. Street lights went out across Oahu. Satellites failed in the months after. And here's the part that gets me. The newspapers announced it, so people threw rooftop parties to watch. A nuclear bomb in space, as a night out."
 },
